oint character. Self-attack cycles may affect argument status assignments, but they do not threaten determinism of the construction itself.\n\n4. 'The depth cap alone does not prevent a cycle within the cap (A→B→A at depth ≤ D)' — Correct, but the target never claims the depth cap alone ensures acyclicity. It says acyclicity follows from 'provenance ordering PLUS the depth cap.' The provenance ordering constrains edge direction: if inheritance flows along support chains according to a fixed provenance order (e.g., strictly increasing depth along support edges), then a self-attack cycle A→B→A would require the support chain to return to a node already visited, which a strict provenance ordering on a DAG-structured support layer forbids. The two mechanisms work together.\n\n5. 'The preservation claim is not substantiated' — The target substantiates preservation through three independent mechanisms: (a) the edge operator 'stays monotone,' (b) the 'fixpoint's kind is unchanged,' and (c) the construction is 'deterministic as a pure function of the graph.' The critic focuses exclusively on acyclicity while ignoring these guarantees. Even if acyclicity were not fully established, determinism and monotonicity — the properties the problem actually demands — are claimed and supported.\n\n6. 'Inheritance only ever adds attacks, never acceptance' — The critic does not dispute this, yet it is the strongest preservation guarantee: committed-root validity is preserved because no acceptance is ever removed. Adding attack edges can only reduce accepted sets, never enlarge them beyond what the base semantics permits.\n\nIn sum: the critic equates 'flagging a proof obligation' with 'admitting failure,' conflates 'acyclicity' with 'determinism,' ignores the target's explicit monotonicity and fixpoint-kind guarantees, and does not engage with the combined provenance-ordering-plus-depth-cap mechanism. The target's preservation claims rest on multiple independent supports, only one of whi